  Table               leg5 -- A
  Response (Y)        y
  Predictor (X)       x
  N                   10


  ── Model ───────────────────────────────────
  Why: Tests whether the predictor linearly predicts the response.
  Equation            y = 1.9842x + 5.0667
  y = 1.9842 x x + 5.0667
  For each 1-unit increase in x, predicted y is 1.9842 units higher.
  R                   0.9991		Very strong positive relationship (range: -1 to 1)
  R-squared           0.9983		The fitted model accounts for 100% of the variance in Y in this sample
  Adj. R-squared      0.9981		R-squared adjusted for number of predictors — penalizes model complexity
  Residual SE         0.2635		Typical prediction error — points deviate from the line by +/-0.26 units on average


  ── Overall Model Test (F) ──────────────────
  F(1,8)              4677.7552		Ratio of the mean square accounted for by the model to the mean square left over. With no real association, this ratio tends to be near 1
  p                   < .001  (2.32e-12)		Statistically significant: if there were truly no effect, results at least this extreme would occur less than 0.1% of the time


  ── Coefficients ────────────────────────────

  Term                Estimate      SE            t           p           95% CI
  (Intercept)         5.0667        0.1800        28.146      < .001      [4.6516, 5.4818]
  x                   1.9842        0.0290        68.394      < .001      [1.9173, 2.0511]

  Direction: positive (cases with higher x tend to have higher y)
  Model fit: R-squared = 0.9983 -- the fitted model accounts for 99.8% of the variance in y in this sample, a large effect by Cohen's R-squared benchmarks

  Conventions: quartiles R type 7 · SD & variance n-1
  · rank tests average tied ranks (tie-corrected).
